{ ===========================================================================
  Psycho Neurosis (Asphyxia, 1994) -- NEUROSIS.006 scene 2, segment 100f.

  A grid of 25 x 25 animated tiles, laid out on a rotating rectangular mesh
  and scrolled up the screen. Every tile plays the same 72-frame animation,
  but which of two colour maps it uses comes out of a cell grid, and where it
  lands comes out of a table of 72 pre-rotated positions.

  ALL THE MOTION IS PRECOMPUTED. `BuildTables` turns 72 angles into 72 x 26 x
  16 pairs of screen coordinates before anything is drawn, so the frame loop
  itself has no arithmetic in it beyond a table index.

  THE TWO .CEL FILENAMES ARE DEAD. `LoadCel` takes a name -- 'p5.cel' and
  'p8.cel' -- copies it into a local, and never looks at it again: the 256
  bytes come from the NEUROSIS.DAT handle the caller already has open. The
  strings are still in the code segment at 100f:00fc and 100f:0103.

  Read from the binary instruction by instruction; nothing here is inferred.
  See docs/27-part6-notes.md for the reading.

  ADDRESS MAP
    Blit          100f:0000     LoadCels      100f:010a
    LoadCel       100f:00ac     Rad           100f:0133
    BuildTables   100f:0173     Setup         100f:03f8
    Run           100f:0590     Scene2        100f:07d5

  DGROUP
    DS:$0002/$0004  the horizontal clip bounds   -- initialised data
    DS:$0006/$0008  the vertical clip bounds
 the cell grid, 170 x 16      -- GetMem 2720
 Cels : array[1..2] of 256 bytes, two colour maps
 the Y table, 72 x 26 x 16 words   -- GetMem 59904
 the X table, likewise            -- GetMem 59904
 the animation, 72 frames of 25 x 25 -- GetMem 45000
 the file
 VirtScrSeg      DS:$96da  the row table
  =========================================================================== }

{$S-}   { opens ENTER $946,0 with no stack check. }
{$G+}   { 186: the shifts take immediates }
{$N+}   { six-byte Reals through the x87 }

unit P6TILES;

{$A-}   { NO VARIABLE ALIGNMENT, and Run's frame is the witness. Pix sits at
          the ODD [BP-$11] -- 100f:05e6 LEAs the tile array at [BP-$4FB], which
          fixes everything above it -- and a Word does not land on an odd offset
          under A-plus. With alignment on the compiler stepped Pix to [BP-$12]
          and took the array and the seven words below it with it, whichever
          length the unread filler was given: ten bytes left the frame a byte
          high, eleven left it a byte low, and neither could ever land. }
{$I-}   { no I/O check: the original goes straight on after each file
          call, with no near CALL to the RTL's check routine. }

interface

procedure Scene2;

implementation

uses Crt, VGA;

{$I gen/DATOFS.INC}   { NEUROSIS.DAT offsets and lengths, generated by
                        tools/mkdat.py from assets/NEUROSIS.MAN. Every
                        constant in it is UNTYPED, so including the whole
                        set costs no storage and changes no data image. }

const
  Angles   = 72;                  { counts to $47 }
  GridRows = 26;                  { compares against $1A }
  GridCols = 16;                  { compares against $10 }
  TileSide = 25;                  { count to $19 }
  TilePix  = TileSide * TileSide; { 625 }
  TileRec  = TilePix + 2;         { 627 -- two bytes of width/height first }
  CellRows = 170;                 { counts to $AA }
  AnimSize = Angles * TilePix;    { 45000 -- 100f:0429 }
  TabSize  = Angles * GridRows * GridCols * 2;   { 59904 -- 100f:040b }
  CellSize = CellRows * GridCols;
  DataOfs  = DAT_P6_CMAP2;   { $1781E5 -- 100f:07f8 pushes $0017 then $81E5 }
  Fix      = 8192.0;              { the tables are 16.13 }
  Bias     = $2000;               { and biased by 8192 so they stay positive }

const
  { DS:$0002..$0008, INITIALISED DATA read straight out of the image: a five
    pixel border all round. Blit reads all four directly, which is why they
    are typed constants -- a plain const would fold into the instruction.

    THESE ARE NOT ADDRESSES TO COPY. The binary says MOV AX,[6]; writing that
    literally reads offset 6 of OUR data segment, which is not the clip bound
    and is what turned the screen to rubbish. Same for the row table at
    $96DA, which is the VGA unit's YOfs. }
  ClipLeft   : Integer = 5;
  ClipRight  : Integer = 315;
  ClipTop    : Integer = 5;
  ClipBottom : Integer = 195;

{ ALL TWENTY-SIX BANDS. P6SHAPE.INC held the same table read from the same
 with a shorter length -- sixteen bands, 2,048 bytes -- and its own
  generator no longer exists. Bands 17..26 are never drawn, exactly as part
  003's unused shape slots are never drawn, and leaving them out moved every
  typed constant declared after them: CellShape ended at $080A where the
  original's ends at $0D0A, and CreditText sits at $0D8A.

  THE 128-BYTE GAP AFTER THIS TABLE IS NOT A 27TH BAND, and it is not an unread
  constant either. Both were tested. A 27th band overshoots: it moves every
  variable in the part from four bytes LOW to 124 HIGH, so the real deficit is
  four bytes. And declaring 128 unreferenced bytes as a constant of their own
  changes the load image by exactly nothing, because **Turbo Pascal smart-links
  away a typed constant no code mentions.**

  That last point is worth carrying, because four holes in this corpus HAVE been
  closed by declaring them: an unread byte at [BP-$22D], 128 unread bytes in
  P6WHOOSH's Setup, two at [BP-$104], sixteen inside part 007's chunk header. Every
  one of those is a LOCAL, and locals are allocated whether or not they are
  read. A hole in the CONSTANT region cannot be filled the same way -- whatever
  is missing there is referenced by something, or the linker would have dropped
  it too. }
{$I gen/P6CELL.INC}    { const CellShape : array[1..27,1..16,1..8] of Byte }

type
  TCells  = array[1..CellRows, 1..GridCols] of Byte;
  TCel    = array[0..255] of Byte;
  TCoord  = array[0..Angles - 1, 1..GridRows, 1..GridCols] of Integer;
  TAnim   = array[0..Angles - 1, 0..TilePix - 1] of Byte;
  TTile   = record W, H : Byte; Pix : array[0..TilePix - 1] of Byte end;
  { TWO-DIMENSIONAL, entry by channel. 100f:0543 builds the index once and
    reads the three channels off consecutive displacements; a flat
    Pal[I * 3 + 1] adds the 1 at run time. Part 004's scene palette and part
    005's both turned out to be this shape, which makes three. Same 768 bytes. }
  TPal768 = array[0..255, 0..2] of Byte;
  TPoint  = record X, Y : Integer end;

var
  Cells : ^TCells;
  Cels  : array[1..2] of TCel;
  YTab  : ^TCoord;
  XTab  : ^TCoord;
  Anim  : ^TAnim;
  F     : file;

{ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
the clipped transparent sprite blit. HAND ASSEMBLER, verbatim,
  and SHARED SOURCE: the routine is src/asm/BLITCLIP.INC, because part 001
  scene 4 compiles the same text at 1107:0199. The marker names the routine,
  which is how it stays byte-checked with its header inside the include.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }
{ @asm 006 100f:0000 +170 Blit }
{$I BLITCLIP.INC}

{ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
read one 256-byte colour map.

  `Name` IS NEVER USED. The routine copies it into a 256-byte local and then
  reads from the file the caller already opened. Transcribed because it is
  what the binary does; the two names it is called with are in the code
  segment and are the only trace of whatever the .cel files once were.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }
{ the two of them, in this order. }
procedure LoadCels;

  { NESTED, and the call site proves it. 100f:0112 pushes the slot number and
    then 100f:0114 pushes BP -- a STATIC LINK -- before the near call, and
 is RET 8: four bytes of String pointer, two of slot, two of link.
    That also places the parameters, because the link takes [BP+4]: Slot lands
    at [BP+6] and Name's offset at [BP+8], which is exactly the pair the
    routine reads.

    Declared at unit level it was RET 6 and the parameters were two bytes low. }
  procedure LoadCel(Name : String; Slot : Integer);
  var
    { ENTER $202 is 514: the String parameter's 256-byte copy at [BP-$100],
      Buf's 256 at [BP-$200], and TWO BYTES below that which nothing reads. }
    Buf    : TCel;
    Unread : Integer;             { never referenced }
  begin
    BlockRead(F, Buf, SizeOf(Buf));
    Cels[Slot] := Buf;
  end;

begin
  LoadCel('p5.cel', 2);
  LoadCel('p8.cel', 1);
end;

{ degrees to radians. CS:$0125 is the ten-byte Pi and CS:$012f
  the single 180.0. }
function Rad(D : Real) : Real;
begin
  Rad := D * Pi / 180.0;
end;

{ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
every position the grid will ever be drawn at.

  The grid is 28 x 16 points on a 15-pixel pitch, centred by subtracting 14
  and 8. For each of 72 angles five degrees apart it is rotated and the
  results stored as plain screen coordinates -- 72 x 26 x 16 of them, twice.
  The sine and cosine are held as 16.13 fixed point biased by 8192, so the
  rotate is an integer multiply and a shift.

  Note the grid is built 1..28 but only 1..26 of it is ever rotated or drawn.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }
procedure BuildTables;
var
  A, I, J : Integer;
  SinT    : array[0..Angles - 1] of LongInt;
  CosT    : array[0..Angles - 1] of LongInt;
  G       : array[1..28, 1..GridCols] of TPoint;
begin
  for A := 0 to Angles - 1 do
    CosT[A] := Round(Cos(Rad(A * 5)) * Fix) + Bias;
  for A := 0 to Angles - 1 do
    SinT[A] := Round(Sin(Rad(A * 5)) * Fix) + Bias;

  for I := 1 to 28 do
    for J := 1 to GridCols do
    begin
      G[I, J].X := (I - 14) * 15;
      G[I, J].Y := (J - 8) * 15;
    end;

  for A := 0 to Angles - 1 do
    for I := 1 to GridRows do
      for J := 1 to GridCols do
      begin
        { pushes the X*Sin term and 100f:0307 SUBTRACTS it from the
          Y*Cos term -- that way round. Getting it backwards mirrors the mesh
          about the wrong axis. }
        { THE TRIG TERM IS WRITTEN FIRST AND THE GRID LOOKUP SECOND, which is
          the operand-order rule read off the parked register. Borland computes
          the RIGHT operand first and parks it, so whichever term ends up in
          CX:BX is the one written second. 100f:029d loads G[I,J] with a 2-D
          subscript, CWDs the word to 32 bits, and 100f:02a2 is MOV CX,AX /
          MOV BX,DX -- the GRID term parked. Written `G[I, J].Y * (CosT[A] -
          Bias)` the compiler parks the trig subtraction instead and the two
          halves come out in the other order, two bytes at each of eight sites.
          Multiplication is commutative, so this is spelling, not arithmetic. }
        YTab^[A, I, J] :=
          ((CosT[A] - Bias) * G[I, J].Y) div Bias -
          ((SinT[A] - Bias) * G[I, J].X) div Bias + 100;
        { AND THE COSINE TERM IS WRITTEN FIRST HERE, the other way round from
          YTab above. Borland computes the RIGHT operand of the addition first,
          so whichever term is named SECOND is the one emitted first: the
          original's statement 2 opens with the SINE term at 100f:0343, which
          means the source names it last. Ten bytes, five operand pairs, and
          addition is commutative, so this is spelling. }
        XTab^[A, I, J] :=
          ((CosT[A] - Bias) * G[I, J].X) div Bias +
          ((SinT[A] - Bias) * G[I, J].Y) div Bias + 160;
      end;
end;

{ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
the assets.

  ASSETS  NEUROSIS.DAT $001781E5:
            256 bytes    colour map 2 ("p5.cel")
            256 bytes    colour map 1 ("p8.cel")
            768 bytes    the palette
            45,000 bytes 72 frames of a 25 x 25 animation
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }
procedure Setup;
var
  { ENTER $386 IS 902 BYTES and it decomposes as 128 + 6 + 768. Only three
    slots are referenced -- [BP-$82], [BP-$84], [BP-$86] -- plus the palette
    below them, so the 128 bytes NEAREST BP are declared and never read.

    First declared is nearest BP, which puts that record first, the three
    Integers next at $82, $84 and $86, and Pal last at $386 -- the address this
    file already recorded for it. Declared with Pal first, as it was, Pal landed
    at [BP-$300] and the frame came out $306.

    A file record is what fits, and P6WHOOSH's Load has the same thing in the same
    position, also unreferenced. }
  Unread  : file;                 { never referenced }
  { I, THEN J, THEN A -- and all three offsets are readable, so none of this is
    a guess. The palette loop at 100f:0544 indexes Pal with the counter at
 so I is first. The first cell loop at 100f:0447 puts its inner
    counter at [BP-$84] and that counter is J. And the shape loop's destination
    at 100f:04d9 multiplies [BP-$86] by eight, which is A. Ours had A and J the
    other way round, which is two bytes of displacement on every reference in
    the three-deep shape loop -- the part's largest remaining span. }
  I, J, A : Integer;
  Pal     : TPal768;
begin
  GetMem(Cells, CellSize);
  GetMem(YTab, TabSize);
  GetMem(XTab, TabSize);
  GetMem(Anim, AnimSize);
  LoadCels;

  { every cell starts as map 2. }
  for I := 1 to CellRows do
    for J := 1 to GridCols do
      Cells^[I, J] := 2;

  { and rows 39..166 are overwritten from the compiled-in
    16 x 16 x 8 table at DS:$000A, transposed. Where it is zero the cell keeps
    its 2. The table is initialised data and is in gen/P6SHAPE.INC; an earlier
    pass reached it with `absolute $164E:$000A`, which is the ORIGINAL's data
    segment and reads rubbish in our build. }
  for A := 1 to GridCols do
    for I := 1 to GridCols do
      for J := 1 to 8 do
        if CellShape[A, I, J] <> 0 then
          { THE ORDER IS NOT COSMETIC, AND IT IS THIS WAY ROUND. 100f:04d9
            computes A*8 into AX, moves it to DX, computes J + $1E in AX and
            only then adds DX back. Borland evaluates the RIGHT operand first
            and parks it, so the term it parks -- A*8 -- is the one written
            SECOND, and the sum reads `(J + 30) + A * 8`. Spelled the other way
            round, A * 8 + (J + 30), the compiler parks J+30 instead and the
            two loads come out swapped; that is what this line used to say, on
            a correct reading of the original and a backwards reading of the
            rule. Dropping the parentheses is worse again: A * 8 + J + 30
            parses as ((A*8) + J) + 30, every right operand is simple, and the
            whole sum stays in AX with no DX at all. }
          Cells^[(J + 30) + A * 8, I] := CellShape[A, I, J];

  BlockRead(F, Pal, SizeOf(Pal));
  for I := 0 to 255 do
    SetRGB(I, Pal[I, 0], Pal[I, 1], Pal[I, 2]);

  BuildTables;
  BlockRead(F, Anim^, AnimSize);
end;

{ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
the frame loop.

  Two 25 x 25 tiles are rebuilt every frame: the animation frame supplies an
  index per pixel and the cell's colour map turns it into a colour, with 0
  left transparent. Then the visible band of the cell grid is walked and every
  non-zero cell blits its tile at the precomputed position.

  The band scrolls up two rows a frame. Past row 145 the screen is cleared
  instead of blitted, and at 146 the scene ends -- so the last few frames run
  black on purpose.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }
procedure Run;
var
  I              : Integer;
  J              : Integer;
  { ELEVEN BYTES BETWEEN J AND Pix, and the arithmetic is what finds them.
 opens ENTER $50a = 1,290 and the routine names only three slots:
 the last being Tile[2].Pix at
 The array is indexed IMUL DI,[BP-$02],$273 against a
    displacement of $76E, so an element is 627 bytes and element 1 sits at
which puts element 2's last byte at [BP-$016]. Below BP the
    declarations therefore have to fill $01..$15 exactly, and Pix at [BP-$11],
    Src at [BP-$13] and Dst at [BP-$15] leave $05..$0F -- eleven bytes -- with
    nothing to put in them. Pix at an ODD offset is the tell: a word does not
    land there unless an odd number of bytes was declared before it. }
  Unread         : array[1..11] of Byte;   { never read
                                             -- ELEVEN, which is the count the
                                             note above works out. Declared as
                                             ten it put Pix at the EVEN [BP-$10]
                                             and moved the tile array and the
                                             seven words below it one byte up,
                                             closing the frame at $508 instead
                                             of $50A. }
  Pix            : Word;
  Src            : Word;
  Dst            : Word;
  Tile           : array[1..2] of TTile;
  AnimSeg        : Word;
  DGroup         : Word;
  CelsOfs        : Word;
  TileSeg        : Word;
  TileOfs        : Word;
  ScrollY        : Integer;
  Frame          : Integer;
begin
  for I := 1 to 2 do
  begin
    Tile[I].W := TileSide;
    Tile[I].H := TileSide;
  end;
  ScrollY := 1;
  Frame   := 0;
  { CRT'S DirectVideo, TURNED OFF, and the identification came from its other
    two sites rather than from here. The byte at DS:$986C is written three times
    in the original and our build already had two of them, because they are
    Crt's own: 1217:0129 sets it to 1 in that unit's initialisation, alongside
    the window and text-attribute globals it sets at $986D..$9878, and
 tests it against 0 inside the character-output loop -- CMP, then
    JNE to a different routine, with the fall-through doing MOV AL,ES:[DI] /
    CALL / INC DI / LOOP. A Boolean at the head of Crt's data, set true by its
    init and read to choose between writing screen memory directly and going
    through the slow path, is DirectVideo and nothing else.

    Turning it off matters in mode 13h: a Write that goes straight to text
    memory lands in the middle of the graphics screen. }
  DirectVideo := False;

  AnimSeg := Seg(Anim^);
  DGroup  := DSeg;
  CelsOfs := Ofs(Cels);
  TileSeg := SSeg;
  TileOfs := Ofs(Tile);

  repeat
    ClearScreen(VirtScrSeg, 0);
    FillChar(Tile[1].Pix, TilePix, 0);
    FillChar(Tile[2].Pix, TilePix, 0);

    for I := 1 to 2 do
    begin
      { ------------------------------------------------------------------
        EQUIVALENT PASCAL, for reference -- the assembler is what runs:

            for K := 1 to 625 do
            begin
              B := Anim^[Frame, K];
              if B <> 0 then Tile[I].Pix[K] := Cels[I][B];
            end;

        It cannot be written that way: DS is swung between the animation and
        DGROUP once per pixel, SI is the running index into both, and BOTH
        loop counters live in registers -- BX across and DX down. An earlier
        pass used DX for the frame base as well as the counter, which is why
        the screen came out as rubbish.
        ------------------------------------------------------------------ }
      { the two bases, worked out the way the original does it:
        by repeated addition rather than a multiply. }
      asm
        PUSH DS
        MOV  CX, TileSeg
        MOV  ES, CX            { ES:DI is the tile being built    }
        XOR  DI, DI
        MOV  CX, I
        DEC  CX
        JZ   @@AtTile
      @@TileStep:
        ADD  DI, 273h          { 627 bytes per tile record        }
        DEC  CX
        JNZ  @@TileStep
      @@AtTile:
        ADD  DI, 2             { past the width/height bytes      }
        ADD  DI, TileOfs

        XOR  SI, SI
        MOV  CX, I
        DEC  CX
        JZ   @@AtCel
      @@CelStep:
        ADD  SI, 100h          { 256 bytes per colour map         }
        DEC  CX
        JNZ  @@CelStep
      @@AtCel:
        MOV  Src, SI
        MOV  SI, CelsOfs
        ADD  Src, SI           { -> Cels[I]                       }

        XOR  SI, SI
        MOV  CX, Frame
        CMP  CX, 0
        JZ   @@AtFrame
      @@FrameStep:
        ADD  SI, 271h          { 625 bytes per animation frame    }
        DEC  CX
        JNZ  @@FrameStep
      @@AtFrame:
        MOV  Dst, SI           { -> this frame                    }

        XOR  SI, SI            { the running pixel index          }
        XOR  DX, DX            { DX counts the 25 DOWN            }
      @@Outer:
        XOR  BX, BX            { BX counts the 25 ACROSS          }
      @@Inner:
        MOV  DS, AnimSeg
        INC  SI
        PUSH SI
        MOV  AX, SI
        MOV  Pix, AX
        ADD  SI, Dst           { + the frame's base               }
        XOR  AX, AX
        MOV  AL, [SI]          { the animation byte               }
        CMP  AL, 0
        JZ   @@Next            { 0 stays transparent              }
        MOV  DS, DGroup
        XOR  SI, SI
        ADD  SI, AX
        ADD  SI, Src           { -> Cels[I][B]                    }
        MOV  AL, [SI]
        PUSH DI
        ADD  DI, Pix
        STOSB
        POP  DI
      @@Next:
        POP  SI
        INC  BX
        CMP  BX, 19h
        JNZ  @@Inner
        INC  DX
        CMP  DX, 19h
        JNZ  @@Outer
        POP  DS
      end;
    end;

    { the visible band of the cell grid. }
    for I := 1 to GridRows do
      for J := 1 to 14 do
        if Cells^[I + ScrollY, J + 1] <> 0 then
          Blit(@Tile[Cells^[I + ScrollY, J + 1]],
               XTab^[Frame, I, J], YTab^[Frame, I, J], VirtScrSeg);

    if ScrollY < CellRows then
      ScrollY := ScrollY + 2;
    if ScrollY < $91 then
      CopyScreen(VirtScrSeg, VGASeg)
    else
      ClearScreen(VGASeg, 0);

    Frame := (Frame + 1) mod Angles;
  { ONE BYTE HERE IS A FLOOR, and Ghidra's boundaries make it exact rather than
    guessed. The original is

        07b5  08 c0        OR   AL, AL
        07b7  74 02        JZ   07bb        KeyPressed false -> test ScrollY
        07b9  eb 0b        JMP  07c6        KeyPressed true  -> the LEAVE
        07bb  81 be f9 fa 92 00   CMP [BP-$507], $92
        07c1  7d 03        JGE  07c6
        07c3  e9 28 fe     JMP  06de        back to the top
        07c6  c9 c3        LEAVE / RET

    IT IS AN Exit, AND THE ARGUMENT THAT SAID OTHERWISE WAS THE WRONG TEST.
    That argument ran: both jumps land on 07c6, whereas in P1TITLE's ShowWord the
    two went to different places, so convergence proves this is a plain
    `until A or B`. Convergence proves nothing here. This `until` is the LAST
    statement of the routine, so `Exit` and falling out of the loop have the
    SAME destination by construction -- the leave/ret at 07c6 -- and both
    readings predict exactly what was measured. ShowWord's divergence was
    positive evidence FOR an Exit; convergence is not evidence against one.

    WHAT DOES DISTINGUISH THEM IS THE SHAPE, and it always did. TP7's `or` is
    short-circuit by default, so `until A or B` tests A and jumps STRAIGHT out
    on true -- one JNE. `if A then Exit` has to jump over the Exit's own JMP,
    which is JZ +2 / JMP, four bytes. The original has four bytes. Three
    earlier attempts looked for a spelling of the `or` that produced the longer
    form; there is none, because the longer form is not an `or`. }
    if KeyPressed then Exit;
  until ScrollY >= $92;
end;

{ the scene. }
procedure Scene2;
begin
  Assign(F, 'neurosis.dat');
  Reset(F, 1);
  Seek(F, DataOfs);
  Setup;
  Run;
  Close(F);
  FreeMem(Cells, CellSize);
  FreeMem(YTab, TabSize);
  FreeMem(XTab, TabSize);
  FreeMem(Anim, AnimSize);
end;

{ the unit's initialisation is empty. }
begin
end.
